|> Section
    handle=_ch6_start_

    *Terminology: “dimension” vs. “dimensional”.*
    As explained in the [Postcript to Chapter >>slopes#decoy:2](>>units_vs_dimensions), 
    the term

    __dimension__

    can mean a qualitative category of units, such as
    in the phrase “$x$ has dimensions of time”.
    On the other hand,

    __dimensional__

    means “that which relates to
    dimensions _or_ units”, 
    i.e., there is no standalone term for
    “that which relates to units”, so we have this catch-all instead.

|> Section
    *Function signatures.*
    If $f$ is a function from $\rr$ to $\rr$
    we write

    $$
    f : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}]
    $$

    to indicate that inputs passed to $f$ have semantics
    of units A while outputs returned by $f$ have semantics
    of units B. 
    For example,

    $$
    h : [\te{kg}] \ra [^\circ\te{F}]
    $$

    indicates that $h$ is a function for which the inputs
    denote kilograms and for which the outputs denote degrees
    fahrenheit. We refer to the ordered pair $(\te{A}, \te{B})$ 
    as the

    __signature__

    of $f$.

    |> Example

        If

        $$
        z : [\te{m}/\te{s}^2] \ra [\rt{0.1}\te{£}\rt{0.1}]
        $$

        then
        
        $$z : \rr \ra \rr$$
        
        is a function
        whose inputs denote meters per seconds squared
        (a measure of acceleration)
        and whose outputs denote British pounds. 

|> Section
    split_here=Rest

    *Dimensionless units.*
    There is a special unit that we denote

    $$
    \Large [1]
    $$

    that stands for a “dimensionless unit” or “dimensionless
    ratio” that, conceptually, arises when a quantity
    is divided by a like-dimensioned quantity. For example,

    __radians__

    are defined as “length over length” in the $xy$-plane—specifically,
    arc length divided by radius—and are one example
    of quantities that carry units of “$[1]$”.
    The outputs of sin, cos, etc, are other examples
    of dimensionless quantities,
    being ratios of the form
    “opposite over adjacent”, etc.
    Thus

    $$
    \cos : [1] \ra [1]
    $$

    where the first ‘$[1]$’ 
    stands for a ratio of a curved length
    divided by a straight length—the radian—and the second
    ‘$[1]$’ stands for a ratio
    of one straight length by another*—the afore-mentioned
    “opposite over adjacent”. (Or “adjacent over hypotenuse”,
    in the case of cosine!)

    |> ImageLeft
        src=images/c6_radian_dimensionless_ratio_cloud.svg

    |> Pause

    _*Note._ To be precise, these “lengths” are actually
    coordinate changes that may be negative—except for the
    hypotenuse-a.k.a.-radius, which is always positive—so they
    are “signed lengths”, or _displacements_, more accurately. 
    Likewise the radian carries a sign—the radian says “rotate by
    this multiple of the radius, with ‘$+$’ for counterclockwise and ‘$-$’
    for clockwise\*\*”.

    |> Pause

    _\*\*Second Note._ To get into the weeds—re: clockwise
    vs. counterclockwise—what counts as the “positive” direction
    of rotation is actually the direction _from the positive $x$
    axis to the positive $y$ axis_:

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_true_counterclockwiseness_1.svg

    What we mean is that if it was our custom to draw the
    $y$ axis pointing downwards, but still draw the $x$ axis
    pointing rightward, the “positive” direction of rotation
    would be clockwise, not counterclockwise:

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_true_counterclockwiseness_2.svg

    (This actually happens inside of SVG, the web-based
    standard for so-called “vector graphics”, in which the $y$
    axis points downwards; in an SVG file, 
    |> span
        class=code-cartouche
        rotate(10)
    \ rotates objects by 10$^\circ$ _clockwise_.)

    |> ImageRight
        src=images/c6_SVG_cloud.svg

|> Section
    *Restrictions imposed on signatures by function
    operations.*
    When we add two functions, the same input is passed
    to both functions:

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_equal_inputs.svg
        style=margin-bottom:-1.3em

    This means that for the sum to make sense, $f$ and
    $g$ need to have the same units of input, i.e.,

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{C}] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    would be the most general signature 
    that one could hope for, in order for the sum to not
    be nonsencial; but this is not stringent enough yet,
    since we actually need

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    in order not to add apples to oranges, 
    at the output end, either, i.e., to avoid 
    “$f(x) =$ apples”, “$g(x) =$ oranges”.

    By contrast, when multiplying two functions we can
    stomach the output units to be different since
    composite units such as “kilowatt-hour”, “worker-years”, etc,
    exist, and likewise when dividing. We have:

    |> Image
        handle=table_1_1 Table 1.1
        src=images/c6_flowers_here_bob.svg

    ...summarizing the constraints that appear at the
    input (“component signatures”) and output (“resultant
    signature”) ends, for the five main function operations.

|> Section

    *The unary function operations.*
    We will add two more not-yet-discussed—or _hardly_* discussed—

    __unary__

    function operations to our list of officially
    considered function operations, being _negation_
    and _reciprocal_, defined by formulas here:

    |> Image
        handle=table_1_2 Table 1.2
        src=images/c6_flowers_here_two_more_v2.svg

    [*See [Chapter >>trigonometry#decoy:5 §>>trigonometry-derivatives#decoy:2](>>negation-of-a-function-first-mention).]

    !! $$
    !! (-f)(x) = -f(x)\qquad(1/f)(x) = 1/f(x)
    !! $$

|> Section
    _handle=derivative_signature
    *Differentiation.*
    As reviewed in [Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4](>>units_of_the_derivative)
    (see also Exercise >>_complete_the_missing_units_#decoy:6 of Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4),
    differentiation divides the units of the output
    by the units of the input. 
    In terms of signatures,
    $$
    f : [\tA] \ra [\tB]
    $$
    if and only if
    $$
    f' : [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA]
    $$
    for any units A, B.
    By the same token, the “if and only if”-ness
    implies that
    $$
    f' : [\tC] \ra [\tD]
    $$
    if and only if
    $$
    f : [\tC] \ra [\tC\tD]
    $$
    for any units C, D. 
    !! (Think that C is playing the
    !! role of A, while D plays the role of B/A.)
    (Think of the fact, as in part “$g$” of
    Exercise >>_complete_the_missing_units_#decoy:6
    of Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4, that one needs to
    multiply the output units of $g''$ by the
    units of input units of $g''$ in order to recover the output
    units of $g'$, etc.)
    
|> Section
    *Operators.*
    On a conceptual note,
    we can view differentiation
    as a function-that-takes-functions-and-outputs-functions,
    the “bigger fish” of the function world. As mathematicians
    use the term

    __operator__

    to denote a function that takes other functions as inputs,
    we therefore speak of the

    __differentiation operator__

    depicted here as an eye-of-Godzilla flying saucer: 

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_differentiation_operator_intro_v2.svg

    Though similarly, the
    unary function
    operations from [Table 1.2](>>table_1_2) 
    can be viewed as operators, taking one function
    as input and producing one function as output:

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_negation_operator_v2.svg

    The other function operations from Table 1.1
    are also operators, albeit

    __binary__

    operators since they take two instead of one
    functions as input. For example, function addition
    can be depicted as a two-input-tube-flying-saucer:

    |> Image
        !! src=images/c6_addition_operator_standalone.svg
        src=images/c6_addition_operator_standalone_v2.svg

    In this fashion, the complete list of all
    operators that we have dealt with so far, similarly
    presented by order of appearance in this chapter, would be
    the following:

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_all_our_operators_v2.svg

    (We recycle ‘$-$’ for two
    different operators—a unary one and a binary one.)
    (Or, as computer scientists would say, we have
    _overloaded_ ‘$-$’.)

    The following figure summarizes the interaction
    between the various operators and function signatures,
    in a similar vein of diagram:

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_operators_acting_on_signatures_v2.svg

    |> StarDivider
        style=margin-top:-0.3em

|> Section
    *The sum rule.*
    The _sum rule_ states that

    |> Boxed
        $$
        (f + g)' = f' + g'
        $$

    for all differentiable functions $f$, $g : \rr \ra \rr$.
    We can apply

    __dimensional analysis__

    to the sum rule, which is to say that we would
    like to “check the units” on the rule, which is to
    say that for two functions with signatures for which
    the left-hand side makes sense, the right-hand side should
    also make sense and vice-versa,
    and the right-hand side should also yield the same
    final signature as the left-hand side.
    !! in all cases.
    !! and yield the same final signature.



    !! |> Section
    !!     *The sum rule.*
    !!     We can apply dimensional analysis to the 
    !!     so-called _sum rule_, which is the identity

    !!     |> Boxed
    !!         $$
    !!         (f + g)' = f' + g'
    !!         $$

    !!     that holds for differentiable functions $f$, $g$.
    !!     !! the _sum rule_. 
    !!     (Exercise >>_sum_rule_summary_#decoy:14, Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4.)
    !!     !! I.e., “check the units” on this equation.

    !!     !! This is a two-step process: we first check that
    !!     We apply a two-step process, first checking that

    !!     __the same constraints 
    !!     on the signatures of $f$ and $g$ are imposed
    !!     by either side of the equation__

    !!     and then checking that, 
    !!     for $f$ and $g$ meeting those constraints, 
    !!     the two sides of the equation come out to the
    !!     same units. We seek perfect agreement between both
    !!     sides of the equation in any and all matters units-related!

    On the left-hand side,

    $$
    f + g
    $$

    imposes signatures of the form

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    as listed in >>table_1_1,
    while the subsequent taking of a derivative doesn't
    impose any new constraints. 
    !! This is the sole
    !! constraint coming from the left-hand side.
    !! so we find that
    !! $f$ and $g$ must have the same input and output
    !! units, as the sole constraints coming from the left-hand
    !! side.

    !! To analyze 
    On
    the right-hand side,
    we start by adopting the agnostic point of view that

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{C}] &\ra [\te{D}] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    for some arbitrary units A, B, C, D; then,

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    f' : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}/\tA] \\
    g' : [\te{C}] &\ra [\te{D}/\tC] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    and the fact that we are taking the sum

    $$
    f' + g'
    $$

    implies 

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    \tA &= \tC \\
    \tB/\tA &= \tD/\tC \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    [a.k.a., “the input units of $f'$ equal the input units
    of $g'$ and the output units of $f'$ equal the output
    units of $g'$”]
    by Table 1.1 again, which, together, imply 

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    \tA &= \tC \\
    \tB &= \tD \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    ...and we recover the same constraints on $f$ and $g$ as
    exist on the left-hand side!

    !! Having completed the first step, 
    !! Having completed the first part of the two-step process, 

    Assuming now that $f$
    and $g$ have the form

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    as required by either side of the equation,
    we have
    !! then we have, one by one in order, that

    $$
    \begin{gathered}
    f + g : [\te{A}] \ra [\te{B}] \\
    (f + g)' : [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
    f' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
    g' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
    f' + g' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35}
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    by the rules according to which signatures 
    respond to the sum and differentiation operators,
    but in particular
    !! by the usual rules according to which
    !! signatures respond to sums and derivatives,
    !! but in particular

    $$
    (f + g)'
    $$

    is found to have the same signature as

    $$
    f' + g'
    $$

    which indicates that the sum “checks out” insofar
    as units are concerned: nothing untoward has been found!

    |> Example
        handle=_ms_x1_x2_example_
        
        If 

        $$
        x_1, x_2 : \rr \ra \rr
        $$

        are two differentiable functions that give 
        position measured in meters as a function of time 
        measured in seconds,
        i.e., two differentiable functions of the form

        $$
        x_1,\rt{0.2} x_2 : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}]
        $$

        where ‘s’ is seconds and ‘m’ is meters as usual,
        then

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        \,x_1 + x_2   : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}] \\
        \,(x_1 + x_2)' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}]\up{1.35} \\
        \,x_1'      : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}] \up{1.35} \\
        \,x_2'      : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}] \up{1.35} \\
        \,x_1' + x_2' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}] \up{1.35}
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        !! $$
        !! \Large
        !! \begin{gathered}
        !! f' : [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA] \\
        !! g' : [\tA] \ra [\tC/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
        !! f'g' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA^2] \up{1.35} \\
        !! !! (fg)' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
        !! \end{gathered}
        !! $$

        and, in particular,

        $$
        !! (x_1 + x_2)' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}]
        (x_1 + x_2)'
        $$

        has the same signature as
        !! matches the signature of

        $$
        !! x_1' + x_2' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}/\te{s}]
        x_1' + x_2'
        $$

        as in the general analysis of the sum rule with arbitrary
        units A and B from above.

        |> Pause

        _Note on Example >>_ms_x1_x2_example_#decoy:0._
        In the case of Example >>_ms_x1_x2_example_, the sum rule,
        which now reads

        $$
        \,(x_1 + x_2)' = x_1' + x_2',
        $$

        !! in that case, 
        can be verbalized by saying that

        __the rate of change of a sum of two 
        coordinates is the sum of their rates of change__

        or

        __the velocity of a sum of two points
        is the sum of their velocities__

        since in this case the “rate of change” is a velocity.

    |> Example
        handle=differentiability_sum_rule_counterexample

        If

        $$
        x_1, x_2 : \rr \ra \rr
        $$

        are given by

        $$
        x_1(t) = |t| = -x_2(t)
        $$

        for all $t \in \rr$ (meaning that

        $$
        x_2(t) = -|t|
        $$

        in the case of $x_2$) then $$(x_1 + x_2)'$$ is defined
        at $t = 0$ but $$x_1' + x_2'$$ is not. One can best see this 
        directly from the graphs of
        $x_1$, $x_2$, $x_1'$, $x_2'$, etc:

        |> Image
            src=images/c6_all_those_x1x2_graphs.svg

        Thus

        $$
        (x_1 + x_2)' \ne x_1' + x_2'
        $$

        but the “reason”, precisely, is that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are not
        differentiable, as they
        are not differentiable, specifically, at $t = 0$.
        (But this is why we say “for differentiable $f$, $g$”
        when we present the sum rule, or other such rules.)
        !! —it is
        !! a necessary disclaimer to keep the statement accurate.)

    |> Pause

    _Postscript._ Even if $f$ and $g$ are not
    both differentiable it is still 
    true, however, that

    |> Boxed
        handle=postscript-on-x_0
        $$
        (f + g)'(x_0) = f'(x_0) + g'(x_0)
        $$

    at all points $x_0 \in \rr$ _such that $f$ and $g$
    are both differentiable at that specific $x_0$_—this gives
    us a “point by point” version of the sum rule—more granular!
    !! (But you can no longer claim that $(f + g)' = f' + g'$
    !! because the boxed equation above may fail for _some_ [other] $x_0$.)

|> Section

    *The “early afteroon product rule”.*
    Gottfried Leibniz, co-inventor of calculus,
    initially guessed that 

    |> Boxed
        $$
        (fg)' = f'g'
        $$

    !! [disclaimer: “for $f$, $g$ differentiable”]
    which is incorrect, though Leibniz also had the
    distinction of making and correcting this
    guess 
    !! mistake 
    in the span 
    of a single day,
    as we know from his notes,
    whence this equation's moniker.

    For a dimensional analysis of this equation, 
    note that, on the left-hand side,

    $$
    fg
    $$

    restricts $f$ and $g$ to signatures of the form

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
    g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{C}] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    for some units A, B, C, per Table 1.1., which is also 
    the most general type of signature that is compatible with the right-hand side.
    This is “so far so good”!
    !! the type of signature that the right-hand side requires—so far so good!

    However,

    $$
    (fg)' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]
    $$
    |> ImageLeft
        src=images/c6_fg_prime_signature_cloud_v2.svg
        offset-x=0em

    whereas

    $$
    f'g' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA^2]
    $$
    |> ImageRight
        src=images/c6_f_prime_g_prime_signature_cloud_v2.svg
        offset-x=0em

    as is easy to check, and

    $$
    [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA^2] \ne [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]
    $$

    so the rule
    !! “the early afternoon product rule” 
    cannot be correct: different units are produced
    by either side of the equation, for the same
    functions $f$ and $g$.
    !! —paraphrasing, “the units don't
    !! even work out”.
    !! Paraphrasing, 
    !! !! dimensional analysis has shown that

    !! __the units aren't even the same__ 

    !! on either side of the equation!

    |> Example
        handle=_second_x1x2_example_

        As in Example >>_ms_x1_x2_example_
        let

        $$
        x_1,\rt{0.1} x_2 : \rr \ra \rr
        $$

        have signature

        $$
        \,x_1,\rt{0.1} x_2 : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}]
        $$

        !! be two functions that 
        so that $x_1$, $x_2$
        give position measured in meters as a function of time measured in seconds.
        Then

        $$
        x_1x_2 : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}^2]
        $$
        !! be two differentiable functions; 

        !! gives meters-squared-as-a-function-of-time-in-seconds,
        and

        $$
        (x_1x_2)' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}^2/\te{s}]
        $$
        
        whereas

        $$
        x_1'x_2' : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}^2/\te{s}^2]
        $$

        so there is no way these two quantities could
        ever be equal, in general. (For example,
        changing from counting time in seconds to
        counting it in hours, while correspondingly
        modifying $x_1$ and $x_2$ to reflect this
        change, would multiply $(x_1x_2)'$ by $3600$ but
        would multiply $x_1'x_2'$ by $3600^2$—so if by
        some fluke the early afternoon product rule
        held before this change it would certainly\*
        [\*assuming $(x_1x_2)' \ne 0$] not hold after,
        and therefore, the early afternoon product rule
        cannot hold in general.)

        |> ImageLeft
            src=images/c6_x1x2_3600_cloud.svg
            offset-x=1em

        |> Pause

        _Note on Example >>_second_x1x2_example_#decoy:0._
        It might be observed that
        
        $$
        x_1'x_2'
        $$

        has the same dimensions (signature) as

        $$
        (x_1x_2)''
        $$

        in Example >>_second_x1x2_example_—one might conjecture that

        $$
        (x_1x_2)'' = x_1'x_2'
        $$

        from this observation, and one would be wrong—which is to
        show that dimensional analysis is quick to debunk an incorrect
        equation but, in the contravening case, 
        cannot be trusted as a proof—nonetheless,

        $$
        x_1'x_2'
        $$

        _does show up as a term_ in the “true”
        formula for $(x_1x_2)''$, so, in some sense,
        dimensional analysis is not _entirely_ wrong,
        even here!

!! |> Section
!!     *The product rule.*
!!     The real “product rule”, valid for differentiable functions $f$ and $g$, is

!!     |> Boxed

!!         $$
!!         (fg)' = f'g + fg'
!!         $$

!!     as covered in Exercise >>_product_rule_#decoy:19 of Chapter >>derivatives#decoy:4.
!!     To start the dimensional analysis, we can
!!     easily see that both sides of the equation
!!     !! impose the constraint
!!     !! have sound units
!!     !! if and only if 
!!     !! $f$, $g$ have signatures
!!     !! of the form
!!     impose signatures of the form

!!     $$
!!     \Large
!!     \begin{aligned}
!!     f : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{B}] \\
!!     g : [\te{A}] &\ra [\te{C}] \up{1.35}
!!     \end{aligned}
!!     $$

!!     on $f$ and $g$. 
!!     From there,
!!     !! in which case

!!     $$
!!     (fg)' : [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA]
!!     $$

!!     while

!!     $$
!!     \Large
!!     \begin{gathered}
!!     !! f': [\te{A}] \ra [\tB/\tA] \\
!!     !! g' : [\te{A}] \ra [\tC/\tA] \up{1.35} \\
!!     f'g :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \\
!!     fg' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \up{1.35}
!!     !! f'g + fg' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \up{1.35}
!!     \end{gathered}
!!     $$

!!     and

!!     $$
!!     f'g + fg' :  [\te{A}] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \up{1.35}
!!     $$

!!     so $f'g + fg'$ has the
!!     same signature as $(fg)'$, i.e., 
!!     this formula passes the “sniff test” of
!!     dimensional analysis.
!!     !! dimensional
!!     !! analysis gives its _imprimatur_ to this formula!
!!     !! (It “passes the sniff test” of dimensional analysis, put
!!     !! differently.)
!!     !! !! (Which is, indeed, the correct one.)

|> Section
    handle=chain_rule_discovery

    *The chain rule.*
    !! As we have formulas for
    !! $$
    !! (f + g)'
    !! $$
    !! and
    !! $$
    !! (fg)'
    !! $$
    We would we like a formula for

    $$
    (f \circ g)'
    $$

    similar to the sum rule,
    !! or product rule,
    and we can try guessing such a formula using
    dimensional analysis, i.e., from the fact that the
    formula should be dimensionally consistent.
    !! and we can collect some candidate(s) for
    !! this formula just by knowing how the units should
    !! behave, i.e., just from the fact that the final
    !! formula should be dimensionally consistent.

    !! and we already know that such a formula will
    !! have to be “dimensionally consistent” (i.e., survive
    !! the dimensional analysis x-ray that we will apply
    !! to it later), so we can also have a try
    !! at guessing the formula just from that fact.

    !! Being bold, we can try to use dimensional
    !! analysis to guess a formula for

    !! $$
    !! (f \circ g)'
    !! $$

    !! where $f$, $g$ are differentiable functions.

    To be clear, such a formula should use only the
    “atomic components”

    $$
    f,\,g,\,f',\,g'
    $$

    on its right-hand side. These are the “lego pieces”
    from which we seek to assemble $(f \circ g)'$.

    !! (Actually, this is kind of fun to do on one's own,
    !! so we leave some vertical space as a “spoiler alert”, for
    !! those who would wish to try.)
    !! !! (Actually, this guessing process is kind of fun to do on one's own,
    !! !! so we leave some vertical space as a “spoiler alert” here, for
    !! !! !! those who wish to try..................)
    !! !! those who wish to try.)

    !! |> Image
    !!     src=images/c6_spoiler_alert_diagonal.svg

    To begin with,

    $$
    (f\circ g)'
    $$

    !! only makes sense, as mentioned in Table 1.1, for 
    requires $f$ and $g$ of the form

    $$
    \begin{aligned}
    f &: [\tB] \ra [\tC] \\
    g &: [\tA] \ra [\tB] \up{1.35}
    \end{aligned}
    $$

    (cf. Table 1.1)
    for some units A, B, C, 
    so these are the “initial constraints” on $f$ and
    $g$. The “lego pieces” then
    !! , as well as $(f \circ g)'$, 
    have the following signatures:
    !! for some units A, B, C, in which case

    $$
    \begin{gathered}
    f            :  [\tB] \ra [\tC] \\
    g            :  [\tA] \ra [\tB] \up{1.35} \\
    f'           :  [\tB] \ra [\tC/\tB] \up{1.35} \\
    g'           :  [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA] \up{1.35}
    !! (f \circ g)' :  [\tA] \ra [\tB\tC/\tA] \up{1.35}
    \end{gathered}
    $$

    Or in more amusing pictorial form (where we
    schizophrenically put input tubes on the left, again):

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_lego_pieces.svg

    From these pieces we seek to assemble a function
    of signature

    $$
    [\tA] \ra [\tC/\tA]
    $$

    which is not a signature of any of the lego pieces;
    but since

    $$
    !! [\tC/\tA] = [\tC/\tB]\cdot[\tB/\tA]
    [\tC/\tA] = \left[{\tC\over \tB}\right]\cdot\left[{\tB\over\tA}\right]
    $$

    we might multiply $f'$ and $g'$;
    and yet the product
    !! except that the product
    $f'g'$
    _cannot be formed_ since $f'$ and $g'$ have different
    input units!; 
    !! oh, what to do?; 
    !! the stroke of genius 
    the answer is to pre-process inputs to $f'$ via $g$,
    i.e., take
 
    $$
    (f'\circ g)g'
    $$

    !! instead of $f'g'$, 
    (a product of functions),
    which makes sense since on the one hand

    $$
    (f'\circ g)
    $$

    is a well-formed composition of signature

    $$
    [\tA] \ra [\tC/\tB]
    $$

    whereas $g'$ has signature

    $$
    [\tA] \ra [\tB/\tA]
    $$

    so that the product of the two has signature

    $$
    [\tA] \ra [\tC/\tA]
    $$

    |> ImageRight
        src=images/c6_chain_rule_units_cloud.svg
        offset-x=3.1em

    !! $$
    !! \Large\left[{\tC\over\tB}\right]\cdot\left[{\tB\over\tA}\right] = \left[{\tC\over\tA}\right]
    !! $$

    as desired! It turns out that this is the
    correct formula, i.e., 
    we truly do have..

    |> Boxed
        $$
        (f \circ g)' = (f'\circ g)g'
        $$

    ...for all differentiable functions $f$, $g : \rr \ra \rr$,
    a formula known as the eponymous _chain rule_$\rt{0.1}$!!

    |> Example
        Say that a cute

        |> Image
            src=images/c6_solo_RAT.svg

        is running a fundraising race where one function

        $$
        f : \rr \ra \rr
        $$

        gives the amount of money raised in dollars as a function
        of the position 
        meters, whereas 
        another function

        $$
        g : \rr \ra \rr
        $$

        !! gives position-reached-in-meters-as-function-of-time-elapsed-in-seconds,
        gives the rat's position in meters
        as a function of time elapsed since the
        start of the race, measured in seconds.
        !! referring 
        !! !! to the position of this particular rat and 
        !! to the time elapsed since
        !! the start of the race.
        !! for this particular rat.
        !! (the time since the start of race, that is).
        I.e.,

        $$
        \begin{gathered}
        f : [\te{m}] \ra [\te{\$}] \\
        g : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}] \up{1.35} 
        \end{gathered}
        $$

        !! $$
        !! f : [\te{m}] \ra [\te{\$}]
        !! $$

        !! and

        !! $$
        !! g : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{m}]
        !! $$

        and

        $$
        f\circ g : [\te{s}] \ra [\te{\$}]
        $$

        !! gives money-raised-dollars-as-a-function-of-time-elapsed-in-seconds.
        gives the money raised, in dollars, as a
        function of time elapsed, in seconds.
        Perforce,

        $$
        (f\circ g)': [\te{s}] \ra [\te{\$}/\te{s}]
        $$

        !! gives the fundraising-rate-in-dollars-per-second-as-a-function-of-time-elapsed-in-seconds.
        !! Also note that
        gives the fundraising rate, in dollars per second,
        !! as a function of time elapsed, measured in seconds, whereas
        as a function of time, in seconds.

        Also note that

        $$
        f' : [\te{m}] \ra [\te{\$}/\te{m}]
        $$

        gives the
        fundraising rate in dollars per meter as a function
        of the position, in meters.
        !! (This could
        !! vary by position: some parts of the course may be “worth”
        !! more than others.)
        !! fundraising-rate-in-dollars-per-meters-as-a-function-of-position-in-meters.
        !! dollars-per-meters as a function of position.

        Then, at $t_0$ seconds after the start of the race,

        $$
        f'(g(t_0))
        $$

        is the dollars-per-meter rate at which the rat is
        fundraising, as we have fed the rat's position $g(t_0)$ into
        $f'$.

        As for the dollars-per-second rate, one can obtain
        that by multiplying the dollars-per-meter rate by the
        rat's meters-per-second velocity, i.e., by taking the product

        $$
        f'(g(t_0))g'(t_0)
        $$

        which is indeed $(f\circ g)'(t_0)$. That is:

        |> Image
            width=530px
            src=images/c6_explained_rat_equation.svg

        Also since

        !! $$
        !! \begin{gathered}
        !! (k\circ \ell)(x) = k(\ell(x)) \\
        !! k(\ell(x)) = (k\circ \ell)(x)
        !! \end{gathered}
        !! $$

        $$
        f'(g(t_0)) = (f'\circ g)(t_0)
        $$
        |> ImageRight
            src=images/c6_composition_reminder_cloud.svg
            offset-x=-1em
        
        we can write the same equation as

        $$
        (f \circ g)'(t_0) = (f'\circ g)(t_0)g'(t_0)
        $$

        or, abstracting the input $t_0$ away, as

        |> Boxed
            $$
            (f \circ g)' = (f'\circ g)g'
            $$

        which is the chain rule as originally stated
        above.
        !! —but to quickly recapitulate
        !! the main point of this example, 
        !! we have
        !! three rates of change...

        !! |> Image
        !!     src=images/c6_three_units_with_arrows.svg

        !! ...and the point is that 
        !! we can obtain 
        !! the former rate of change ($\left[{\te{\$}}\over\te{s}\right]$)
        !! by multiplying the latter two rates
        !! of change
        !! ($\left[{\te{\$}}\over\te{m}\right]$ and $\left[{\te{m}}\over\te{s}\right]$),
        !! we just have to be careful to evaluate $f'$
        !! at the right “place”—we don't want to feed
        !! $f'$ an amount of seconds when it is
        !! expecting an amount of meters!

        !! |> ImageRight
        !!     src=images/c6_forbidden_fprime_of_t0_v2.svg
        !!     line=-3
        !!     offset-x=1em

        |> Pause

        _Note._ Another common way to write the chain rule is

        |> Boxed

            $$
            (f \circ g)'(x) = f'(g(x))g'(x)
            $$

        as previously seen with “$t_0$” in place of “$x$”. 
        !! is a more granular version of the chain rule,
        In this case the assumption 
        
        __for $f$, $g : \rr \ra \rr$ differentiable__
        
        can be replaced by the more granular

        __for $f, g : \rr \ra \rr$, $x \in \rr$ such that $g$ is 
        differentiable at $x$ and $f$ differentiable at $g(x)$__

        similarly to the [postscript](>>postscript-on-x_0) following Example >>differentiability_sum_rule_counterexample,
        relative to the sum rule.
        !! similarly to the sum rule. (Cf. the 
        !! [note after Example >>differentiability_sum_rule_counterexample](>>postscript-on-x_0).)
        !! to the more granular version of the
        !! sum rule, [given after Example >>differentiability_sum_rule_counterexample](>>postscript-on-x_0).

        !! similarly to the 
        !! !! which is like we did for the sum rule in the 
        !! [“Postscript” after
        !! Example >>differentiability_sum_rule_counterexample](>>postscript-on-x_0).

    |> Example
        handle=cos_cos_chain_rule

        We have

        $$
        (\cos \circ \cos)' = ((-\sin) \circ \cos)(-\sin)
        $$
        |> ImageLeft
            src=images/c6_chain_rule_reminder_cloud.svg
            offset-x=2em

        by [the chain rule](>>chain_rule_discovery),
        since $\cos' = -\sin$, or

        $$
        (\cos \circ \cos)'(x) = ((-\sin) \circ \cos)(x)\cdot(-\sin)(x)
        $$

        evaluating at an input $x$, 
        or
        
        $$
        (\cos \circ \cos)'(x) = \sin(\cos x)\sin(x)
        $$

        when evaluating the ‘$\circ$’ and cancelling the minus signs. 
        
    |> Example
        handle=cos_cos_chain_rule_continued
        We have

        $$
        (\cos \circ \cos)'(2.5) = \sin(\cos(2.5))\cdot \sin(2.5) = -0.429\ldots
        $$

        by Example >>cos_cos_chain_rule,
        which looks visually compatible with the graph of
        $\cos(\cos x)$
        since
        $(\cos \circ \cos)'(2.5)$ is supposed to be the slope
        of $y = \cos(\cos x)$ at $x = 2.5$ (after all):

        |> Image
            src=images/c6_sin_of_sin_graph.svg

        |> StarDivider

        _Note on Example >>cos_cos_chain_rule_continued##_. Because

        $$
        -1 \leq \cos(x) \leq 1
        $$
        
        the values of $\cos(\cos(x))$ remain stuck between

        $$
        \cos(\pm 1) = \cos(\pm 57.29\ldots^\circ) \approx 0.54
        $$

        and $\cos(0) = 1$
        which produces the graph above, that one can actually
        think of as the $x$-coordinate of a certain horizontal
        windshield-wiper whose angle at time $t$ is $\cos(t)$:

        |> Image
            src=images/c6_windshield_wiper.svg

        !! $$
        !! \cos(\cos(t))
        !! \cos(\cos t)
        !! $$

|> Section
    !! *Appendix: radians _&_ the dimensionless plane.*
    *Epilogue: the dimensionless plane.*
    Imagine that we define the radian value of an
    angle as

    __length subtended by the angle on the unit circle__

    as opposed to

    __arc length over radius__

    where the latter is the more traditional definition.
    !! In the second case, 
    In the latter definition, 
    the dimensions of the radian are
    
    $$
    {\te{length}\over\te{length}} = \te{dimensionless}
    $$

    whereas under the first definition it sounds like
    the dimensions are simply “length”. BUT: “length”
    _in what plane_? The “original” plane in which the
    unit circle is embedded has dimensionless axes! And
    therefore _length itself_$\rt{0.2}$ in that plane is a dimensionless
    quantity, not the usual “physical” notion of length!
    So both cases assign radians to be a dimensionless 
    unit!
    Whoo-hoo!

    Relatedly to this idea of the original “vanilla”
    $xy$-plane having dimensionless axes, you may
    have seen people label axes this way, writing
    “time/s” instead of “time $[\te{s}]$”, e.g.:

    !! |> Image
    !!     src=images/c6_dimensionless_plane_1_v3.svg
    !!     style=margin-top:-1.4em

    !! |> Image
    !!     src=images/c6_dimensionless_plane_1_v2.svg
    !!     style=margin-top:-1.4em

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_dimensionless_plane_1_v1.svg
        style=margin-top:-1.4em

    The idea behind this notation is that the plane
    itself is a dimensionless object, so by doing

    $$
    {\te{time}\over\te{s}}
    $$

    where “time” is a time measurement in seconds,
    we have “taken the units out of the measurement”
    and obtained a “pure number” that “fits” inside
    the plane; (!); for example, this ‘$3$’ here...

    |> Image
        src=images/c6_dimensionless_plane_1_v1_circled.svg

    ...is really a $3$ without any units—the “pure” $3$—because
    the entire plane is dimensionless, but 
    !! following the axis label, we find...
    !! if we take the axis label at its word, we have...
    !! follow the semantics of the axis label, we find...

    $$
    3 = {\te{time}\over \te{s}}
    $$

    !! ...which implies...
    !! which turns into
    !! with a “pure $3$” 
    with no (i.e., dimensionless) units
    on the left implies

    $$
    3\te{s} = \te{time}
    $$

    or “time equals $3$ seconds”, i.e., that vertical
    slice of the plane corresponds to the moment when
    the “time” variable—which _does_ have units—equals
    $3$ _seconds_, not just ‘$3$’, the pure number!!
        